Charge resides on the outer surface of a conducting hollow sphere of radius $\mathrm{R}$. We consider a spherical surface of radius $r \lt  R$. By Gauss theorem<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fviteee%252FNQX1Rec9_NVMqR5f_SqmKo-Awj6a3p_f_nGViMJ1zlE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fviteee%2FNQX1Rec9_NVMqR5f_SqmKo-Awj6a3p_f_nGViMJ1zlE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fviteee%2FNQX1Rec9_NVMqR5f_SqmKo-Awj6a3p_f_nGViMJ1zlE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media"><br>

\begin{array}{l}
\int_{\mathrm{s}} \overrightarrow{\mathrm{E}} \cdot \mathrm{ds}=\frac{1}{\varepsilon_{0}} \times \operatorname{ch} a r g \mathrm{e} \quad \text { enclosed or } \\
\mathrm{E} \times 4 \pi \mathrm{r}^{2}=\frac{1}{\varepsilon_{0}} \times 0 \Rightarrow \mathrm{E}=0
\end{array}

i.e electric field inside a hollow sphere is zero.

When a dielectric slab of dielectric constant $\mathrm{K}=\frac{3}{2}$ is inserted between the plates of $\mathrm{C}_{2}$ its new capacitance $\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}^{\prime}\right)$ becomes

\mathrm{C}_{2}^{\prime}=\frac{3}{2} \mathrm{C}

Equivalent capacitance of $\mathrm{C}_{2}^{\prime}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ is

\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{eq}}=\mathrm{C}_{2}^{\prime}+\mathrm{C}_{3}=\frac{3}{2} \mathrm{C}+\mathrm{C}=\frac{5 \mathrm{C}}{2}
$<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fviteee%252FMiNQs9OOKPBK8v7jldTXgOrxurWj3xI5yYkysBVlz_Y.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fviteee%2FMiNQs9OOKPBK8v7jldTXgOrxurWj3xI5yYkysBVlz_Y.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fviteee%2FMiNQs9OOKPBK8v7jldTXgOrxurWj3xI5yYkysBVlz_Y.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ 
Now, $\mathrm{C}_{\text {eq }}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{1}$ are in series. Therefore, their equivalent capacitance is

\begin{aligned}
C_{\mathrm{eq}} &=\frac{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{eq}} \times \mathrm{C}_{1}}{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{eq}}+\mathrm{C}_{1}}=\frac{\frac{5 \mathrm{C}}{2} \times \mathrm{C}}{\frac{5 \mathrm{C}}{2}+\mathrm{C}} \\
&=\frac{5 \mathrm{C}^{2}}{7 \mathrm{C}}=\frac{5 \mathrm{C}}{7}
\end{aligned}

Capacitance, $\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{A}}=\frac{\mathrm{K}_{1} \varepsilon_{0} \mathrm{~A}}{\frac{\mathrm{d}}{2}}, \mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{B}}=\frac{\mathrm{K}_{2} \varepsilon_{0} \mathrm{~A}}{\frac{\mathrm{d}}{2}}$
\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{\mathrm{C}_{1}}{\mathrm{C}_{2}}=\frac{2 \mathrm{~K}_{1} \mathrm{~K}_{2}}{\mathrm{~K}_{1}+\mathrm{K}_{2}} \\
=\frac{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{A}} \mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{B}}}{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{A}}+\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{B}}}=\left(\frac{2 \mathrm{~K}_{1} \mathrm{~K}_{2}}{\mathrm{~K}_{1}+\mathrm{K}_{2}}\right) \frac{\varepsilon_{0} \mathrm{~A}}{\mathrm{~d}}\left(\because \mathrm{e}=\frac{\varepsilon_{0} \mathrm{~A}}{\mathrm{~d}}\right)
\end{array}

Magnetic field

\begin{aligned}
B &=\frac{\mu_{0} \varepsilon_{0} r}{2} \frac{d E}{d t}=\frac{1}{9 \times 10^{16} \times 2} \times 10^{10} \\
&=5.56 \times 10^{-8} \mathrm{~T}
\end{aligned}

Capacitance $1 \mu \mathrm{F}$ and $\mathrm{C} \mu \mathrm{F}$ are connected in series,

\therefore \mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{\mathrm{C}}{1+\mathrm{C}}

Given, $\mathrm{V}=120 \mathrm{~V}$ and $\mathrm{q}=80 \mu \mathrm{C}$
\begin{array}{l}
\because \mathrm{q}=\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{eq}} \mathrm{V} \\
80=\frac{\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{C}+1} \times 20 \\
\text { or } \mathrm{C}=2 \mu \mathrm{F}
\end{array}

Energy stored in the capacitor of capicity C

\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{U}=\frac{1}{2} \frac{\mathrm{q}^{2}}{\mathrm{C}} \\
=\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{\left(80 \times 10^{-6}\right)^{2}}{2 \times 10^{-6}} \\
=\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{80 \times 10^{-6} \times 80 \times 10^{-6}}{2 \times 10^{-6}} \\
\mathrm{U}=1600 \mu \mathrm{J}
\end{array}

Energy released on discharge of capacitor

\begin{array}{l}
U=\frac{1}{2} C V^{2} \\
=\frac{1}{2} \times 4 \times 10^{-6} \times(100)^{2}=0.02 \mathrm{~J}
\end{array}

\begin{array}{l}
\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{s}}=\frac{\mathrm{C}_{1} \mathrm{C}_{2}}{\mathrm{C}_{1}+\mathrm{C}_{2}}=3 \\
\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{p}}=\mathrm{C}_{1}+\mathrm{C}_{2}=16 \therefore \mathrm{C}_{1} \mathrm{C}_{2}=48 \\
\begin{array}{r}
\mathrm{C}_{1}-\mathrm{C}_{2}=\sqrt{\left(\mathrm{C}_{1}+\mathrm{C}_{2}\right)^{2}-4 \mathrm{C}_{1} \mathrm{C}_{2}} \\
=\sqrt{16^{2}-4 \times 48}=\sqrt{64}=8 \\
\mathrm{C}_{1}+\mathrm{C}_{2}=16 \mu \mathrm{F} \\
\mathrm{C}_{1}-\mathrm{C}_{2}=8 \mu \mathrm{F} \\
\Rightarrow 2 \mathrm{C}_{1}=24 \mu \mathrm{F} \Rightarrow \mathrm{C}_{1}=12 \mu \mathrm{F} \\
\therefore \mathrm{C}_{2}=\frac{48}{12}=4 \mu \mathrm{F}
\end{array}
\end{array}

$\mathrm{V}=300 \mathrm{~V}, \mathrm{C}_{1}=2.0 \mu \mathrm{F}, \mathrm{C}_{2}=8.0 \mu \mathrm{F}$,
Net capacitance, $\frac{1}{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{s}}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{C}_{1}}+\frac{1}{\mathrm{C}_{2}}$
\begin{array}{l}
\Rightarrow \mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{s}}=\frac{\mathrm{C}_{1} \mathrm{C}_{2}}{\mathrm{C}_{1}+\mathrm{C}_{2}} \\
\Rightarrow \mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{s}}=\frac{2 \times 8}{2+8}=\frac{16}{10}=1.6 \mu \mathrm{F}
\end{array}

Now total charge,
$\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{s}} \times \mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{s}}=300 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-6}=4.8 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{C}\because$ In series charge is same on capacitors
⇒ Charge on $2 \mu \mathrm{F}$ capacitor is $4.8 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{C}$

Consider the potential at D be 'V'. Potential drop across $C_{1}$ is $\left(V-V_{1}\right)$ and $C_{2}\mathrm{i}\left(\mathrm{V}_{2}-\mathrm{V}\right)\therefore \mathrm{q}_{1}=\mathrm{C}_{1}\left(\mathrm{~V}-\mathrm{V}_{1}\right), \mathrm{q}_{2}=\mathrm{C}_{2}\left(\mathrm{~V}_{2}-\mathrm{V}\right)$
As $\mathrm{q}_{1}=\mathrm{q}_{2} \quad$ [capacitors are in series]

\begin{array}{l}
\therefore C_{1}\left(V-V_{1}\right)=C_{2}\left(V_{2}-V\right) \\
V=\frac{C_{1} V_{1}+C_{2} V_{2}}{C_{1}+C_{2}}
\end{array}

Current, $i=q v$
\begin{aligned}
B &=\frac{\mu_{0}^{i}}{2 r}=\frac{\mu_{0} q v}{2 r} \\
&=\frac{4 \pi \times 10^{-7} \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \times 6.6 \times 10^{15}}{2 \times 0.53 \times 10^{-10}} \\
&=\frac{2 \pi \times 1.6 \times 6.6}{5.3}=12.513 \mathrm{~T}
\end{aligned}
